   "take": "Narrower overall, with setbacks. In 2003 Q1, 12 of the 15 CMAs had a gap of 25 pp or more; in 2026 Q1, none did, and the widest gap was 14.8 pp (Ottawa–Gatineau). Against a year earlier, though, the gap widened in 15 of the 15 CMAs in 2021 Q1, the most of any quarter. On the convergence rule, the 2030 Q4 gaps would span 10.0–13.6 pp (2026 Q1: 9.1–14.8 pp); for one CMA the three rules differ by up to 4.4 pp (Toronto), a reminder that the outlook is a projection.",
   "outCard": "About the outlook\nTested from 2012 Q4 on, 19 quarters ahead the rules missed by 2.6 pp (convergence), 2.6 pp (no change) and 5.8 pp (10-year trend) on average.\nThe rules know nothing about future policy, benefit rates, caseload growth or the economy; a change in either program would break them.\nThey disagree most for Toronto: 4.4 pp apart in 2030 Q4.\nA projected gap is not a target, and a narrower gap does not mean that needs converged.",
